<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bishkek</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Frunze, Pishpek</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bishkek is the capital and largest city of Kyrgyzstan, in the Chüy valley at the foot of the Kyrgyz Range. It grew from Pishpek, a fortress of the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> taken by the Russians in 1862. In 1926 it was renamed Frunze, after the Bolshevik commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-frunze">Mikhail Frunze</a>, who was born there, and it served as capital of Soviet Kyrgyzstan. It took the name Bishkek in 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bishkek.jpg" alt="Bishkek" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aitenir · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bishkek_City%27s_business_center.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
